  "summary": "Compute an expression written in postfix form.",
  "statement": "Evaluate the postfix expression `tokens` (e.g. `[\"2\",\"1\",\"+\",\"3\",\"*\"]` is `(2 + 1) * 3`). Operators\nare `+ - * /`; division truncates toward zero. The expression is valid and never divides by zero.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= tokens.length <= 10^4",
    "intermediate values fit in an int"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "tokens = [\"2\", \"1\", \"+\", \"3\", \"*\"]",
      "output": "9"
    },
    {
      "input": "tokens = [\"4\", \"13\", \"5\", \"/\", \"+\"]",
      "output": "6"
    },
    {
      "input": "tokens = [\"10\", \"6\", \"9\", \"3\", \"+\", \"-11\", \"*\", \"/\", \"*\", \"17\", \"+\", \"5\", \"+\"]",
      "output": "22"
    }
  ],
  "signature": {
    "method": "evalRPN",
    "params": [
      {
        "name": "tokens",
        "type": "String[]"
      }
    ],
    "returns": "int"
  },
  "starter_code": "class Solution {\n    public int evalRPN(String[] tokens) {\n        // TODO: write your solution here\n        return 0;\n    }\n}\n",
  "hints": [
    "An operator applies to the two most recent values.",
    "Push numbers on a stack; for an operator, pop `b` then `a` and push `a op b`.",
    "stack = []\nfor t in tokens:\n    if t is an operator:\n        b, a = stack.pop(), stack.pop()\n        stack.append(apply(t, a, b))\n    else: stack.append(int(t))\nreturn stack[0]",
    "O(n). Order matters: the first value popped is the right-hand operand. Java's `/` already truncates toward zero."
  ],
  "expected_complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
